```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: laptop0
diagnosis_text:
Laptop0 has a misconfigured default gateway that points to a non-existent IP
address. The device has a /24 address in VLAN 20 (172.16.20.10/24) and its
default route is statically set to 172.16.20.99 — a host address that is not
assigned to any device in the topology. The actual VLAN 20 inter-VLAN gateway
is router0's sub-interface eth0.20, which correctly holds 172.16.20.1/24.

Because 172.16.20.99 does not exist, any packet destined outside the local
/24 subnet triggers an ARP request for 172.16.20.99 that is never answered.
All off-subnet traffic is silently dropped at laptop0 itself: the host cannot
reach Location C, other router segments, or any other part of the network.
Intra-VLAN traffic (other 172.16.20.x hosts) works normally because it never
needs the default route.

PC4 and PC5 on VLAN 10 are unaffected because they correctly point to
172.16.10.1 (router0 eth0.10), which responds and routes normally.
Direct confirmation: pinging 172.16.20.1 from laptop0 succeeds with < 2 ms
RTT (L2 path through mswitch1 to router0 is healthy), while pinging
172.16.20.99 yields 100% loss.

Root cause: laptop0's startup configuration assigns the wrong default gateway
IP (172.16.20.99 instead of 172.16.20.1).

Corrective fix: Change laptop0's default gateway to 172.16.20.1, e.g.:
  ip route del default; ip route add default via 172.16.20.1 dev eth0
Update the persistent startup script so the fix survives reboots.
=== END ===
```